  <p>Other times, we use one of the recipes below and mix up something extraordinary. Take a look for yourself.</p>

  <h2>What is amaretto?</h2>
  <p>Amaretto is a sweet, almond-flavoured liqueur that originated in Saronno, Italy.</p>
  <p>Depending on the brand, it may be made from apricot kernels, bitter almonds, peach stones or almonds. They’re all natural sources of benzaldehyde, which gives the liqueur its almond-like flavour.</p>
  <p>Amaretto is generally 21 to 28 percent alcohol.</p>

  <h2>Classic amaretto cocktails</h2>
  <p>Classics-wise, we have:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/alabama-slammer/">Alabama Slammer</a>, a far too drinkable cocktail that mixes amaretto, Southern Comfort, sloe gin and orange juice to make something unique… and boozy.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/french-connection/">French Connection</a>, an elegant combination of cognac and amaretto. Yes, that’s all there is to it, and it’s delicious.</li>
    <li><a href="/cocktails/godfather/">Godfather</a>, which, like the French Connection, keeps it simple: Scotch whisky and amaretto make a boozy after-dinner drink.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What amaretto should I use?</h2>
  <p>So, what amaretto should you use for your amaretto cocktails? Easy! Whatever you have in. But if you’re in the market for a new bottle, we recommend Disaronno.</p>

